  "metadata": {
    "title": "From the Master Document to the Software: a tutorial on using the ARKHE OS & AGI specification as a prompt for the complete construction of arkhe-agi",
    "doi": "10.5281/zenodo.22887710",
    "publication_date": "2026-09-22",
    "description": "<p>Large-scale software specifications challenge direct use by coding agents: the Arkhe OS &amp; AGI &mdash;<br>Integrated Master Document (2nd edition) spans 68,262 lines and 2,287,868 characters &mdash; an estimated<br>570&ndash;650 thousand tokens &mdash;, exceeding standard 200 thousand-token context windows. This paper turns<br>that document into an operating manual: the DMCP pipeline (Master-Document-as-Prompt), which slices<br>the corpus into 50 addressable blocks with stable identifiers, prefixes every prompt with a six-principle<br>constitution (fail-closed, honest status, block traceability, one phase at a time, executable evidence, and<br>declared reconciliation of divergent versions), and orders the construction of the arkhe-agi software into<br>seven phases (F0&ndash;F6), from the 15-crate monorepo bootstrap to the MCP server, using executable gates<br>&mdash; cargo check/test/clippy/fmt/deny, the eight gates of the v5 audit, and the governed write-audit-publish<br>protocol of the aia repository &mdash; as acceptance criteria, with logs attached to every commit as evidence.<br>Universal prompt and audit templates are provided, along with an analysis of limitations (context<br>saturation, package and API hallucination, internal corpus divergences) and their mitigations. We<br>conclude that verification, not prompting, is the architecture: block-to-code fidelity is only sustainable<br>when every specification claim becomes an executable test.</p>",
